ction in the muscle  where you're not moving through a range  of motion  so if you've got your dumbbells and held  them for as long as you possibly could  that would be an isometric contraction  and specifically that would be a  yielding isometric contraction which  means that you're holding them for as  long as you can until you start to give  out and your arms start to go down  however you're not using your max  strength at any point because you always  could move them higher  if you're using your max strength say  you were pushing or pulling against them  that couldn't move say you had a chain  attached to the ground and you're trying  to curl it that would be an example of  an overcoming isometric and obviously  this allows you to exert a lot more  strength and therefore it can build more  strength more quickly what's great about  the l-sit in the visa is that it has  properties of both it's a yielding  isometric and that you're holding a  position for as long as you can until  you start to lower the legs but you can  also try and lift the legs as high as  possible attempting that v-sit and  thereby you're making it more of a  overcoming isometric because you're  essentially fighting against the  resistance provided by your own body and  this is where this becomes a fantastic  movement for performance because you're  building something called compressive  strength and that's literally your  ability to compress your own body to  bring your upper torso down towards your  legs the reason this is difficult is not  only because we lack the flexibility but  also because we lack the strength and  that combined limits our mobility so  mobility is the ability to get into a  position without external help so  without someone pushing you so if you  can bend over as far as you can and  touch the ground slap the ground with  your hands that's fantastic mobility and  that's going to help you to kick higher  it's going to reduce pain on your lumbar  spine it's going to translate to  generally better performance all around  it'll improve your squat technique so  this is a fantastic way to not only be  training the abs as in building core  strength and stability rigidity but at  the same time to be developing that  compressive strength that will result in  better performance greater mobility and  just generally better movement on top of  all that there are benefits of isometric  training in general this is a great way  to train strength endurance because  you're holding a position and at the  same time it's great if you're dealing  with an injury or rehabbing because  you're not going through a range of  motion you've reduced the likelihood of  hurting yourself further and just in  case you weren't convinced the l-sit is  also fantastic for developing straight  arm strength this is your ability to  exert strength through locked out  straight arms this again translates to a  range of advanced calisthenics movements  famously the planche it can translate to  a better bench press because you're  developing the seratus muscles and the  scapular protraction it can translate to  reduced likelihood of injury during  movements like the deadlift once again  this is a underrated aspect of strength  and something that everyone can benefit  from training the lcs is a real gentle  introduction of an easy way to train  that and you're getting all these other  benefits so yes everyone i think should  be incorporating l-sits into their  training so that's enough talking about  why you should do the l-sit now it's  time that we actually look at how to do  the l-sit um so basically i'm going to  start by showing you on the floor  because that seems like the most proper  way to do it you want to get into a  normal sitting position with your legs  sticking out and now what you want to do  is put your hands flat on the ground and  twist your elbow pits outwards to keep  them completely straight and locked  that's actually not 100 essential but if  you want to 