e it more  aesthetic but it'll also make it a  little bit easier because you're going  to not also be fighting a stretch on the  calves obviously if you struggle to  perform a l-sit then the easiest  variation is to put your hands up on  something slightly higher up therefore  meaning you'd have to clear quite as  much ground to raise your legs and you  have to be in a full l i'm going to be  using some parallettes here you can use  all sorts of things whether it's two  chairs opposite each other or two  handstand blocks  simply hold on and then raise your legs  another easier variation of course is to  not straighten out the legs but to  simply hold yourself in a tuck position  like this and if you want a tough  progression all you can do is hold  yourself in that l-sit for as long as  you can and when you fatigue  pull into that tuck position and that'll  do it however i think what i would  slightly prefer for most people is that  instead of being in the tuck position  you simply hold yourself in as good a  nail position as you can because this  way you're still working on that  mobility you're still working against  the tension in the antagonistic muscles  and that way we're building towards what  we actually want to achieve of course  another variation is to perform an  assisted l-sit and to do that you can  obviously get a friend to hold your legs  you can use a resistance band that's  hanging off of something or gymnastics  rings or you can simply  rest your feet on the ground slightly  lift them as much as you can  but if you don't clear the ground then  that's fine and it'll get better as you  go so on this channel i always say that  you shouldn't just focus on gaining the  skills it's actually the underlying  traits that are more important so if you  want to be able to do the l-sit or the  v-sit then what you really want to be  working on is that compressive strength  and one way you can do this is with a  move from gymnastic strength training  called the pike pulse so you're going to  sit on the ground like straight out  front of you locked out then you're  going to lean forwards with your hands  and lean slightly forwards trying not to  round the lumbar spine too much and then  you're just going to pulse the legs up  like this and you'll immediately feel it  and as you get better at this you can  lean forwards and forwards i can't what  you want to try not to do is to rock  like this because then you're not  actually working what you're trying to  work  if you stick that into your workouts  that will help you to gain the mobility  and the strength you need a lot quicker  so another move you can do to stretch  your hamstrings that i've really liked  recently is called the elephant walk and  i got this once again from knees over  toes guy put your hands on the floor in  front of you try not to round the lower  back too much and you are allowed to  bend the knees on this occasion and what  you're going to do then is you're going  to take it in turns to straighten one of  the knees and then you're going to feel  that hamstring stretch on that side and  what you're going to do over time is  bring the hands closer to you as you are  able to and this is a great way to  really rapidly improve your hamstring  mobility and the great thing about this  one is that because you're only moving  one leg at a time you're going to reduce  the tendency there to round that lower  back which can sometimes prevent you  from getting the full stretch on the  hamstring sometimes it can also lead to  a bit of unwanted pain there one thing  you can do of course if you can't get  into an l-sit or a visa is to swing your  legs into that position and then to  control the descent as much as possible  and that's a really good way to hack  your way into this essentially like  doing a cheat with a dumbbell curl  of course one of the most famous and  well-known variations is the v-sit this  is where you're going to be bringing  your legs up a lot higher and what you  can do here is to bring your hips  forward slightly and at the same time it  is okay to bend your arms slightly if  you need to lean back into it more this  is obviously going to really challenge  that compressive strength this is a  really difficult move to do and you of  course shouldn't expect to be able to  just launch straight into a v-sit so  very few people can do it  grant basically to get here all you want  to do is keep practicing your l-sit make  sure you're actively trying to raise  those legs not just sitting there  passively and then if you do your pike  pulses and your elephant walks on top of  that any other hamstring stretches  you'll get there eventually  i hope obviously another really cool  variation you can do is the l-sit to  handstand and as the name suggests  you're going from an l-sit into a  handstand by doing this you get all the  benefits of the l-sit and all the  benefits of a handstand you can add a  little handstand press in there and this  makes a hybrid movement obviously it's a  tricky movement to build up to if you  practice the l-sit and the handstand  separately eventually you can connect  them what you want to do is have a  little bit of momentum going out of the  l-sit or the v-sit to help carry you up  try not to bend the arms too much and  that'll minimize the amount of handstand  press you need to do and another  variation you can do of course is to  just put some kind of ankle weights on  or you can even hold a medicine ball  between your feet and conversely as  grant just pointed out if you take your  shoes off when you're practicing the  l-sit and v-sit actually make it easier  and you can get even higher  thanks so much for watching guys i hope  you found this video useful and  interesting if you did then please leave  a like and share around that'll help me  out immensely let me know if you do  l-sits in the comments and what your  favorite variations are thanks again to  grant stevens for helping me o